## The design rule

> The LLM orchestrates. It never makes a clinical decision.

<Flow steps={[
  { label: 'Patient conversation', detail: 'The model phrases questions and extracts answers from free text under a forced JSON schema', kind: 'model' },
  { label: 'Widget-only fields', detail: 'Safety-critical answers can be set only through interface controls, under a default-deny allowlist — never from a sentence the model interpreted', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Plausibility gate', detail: 'Physical measurements checked before they reach the engine. A transposed height is not a clinical question, but it produces a clinical answer', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Deterministic engine', detail: 'Eligibility matrix, interaction rules and the universal safety gate. Pure code, exhaustively testable' },
  { label: 'Backend forces the verdict', detail: 'The model cannot declare itself finished. A model that can announce completion can announce the wrong completion', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Recommend · route to a doctor · stop' }
]} note="Orange = the model's part. Black = a deterministic gate. The second version moved the order of questions out of the model and into code, narrowing its role further." />

The pattern that generalises: use the model for what it is good at — understanding messy human
text and producing fluent replies — and keep the state machine in code, where it can be tested
exhaustively.

## Validating a change to clinical logic

This is where the engineering gets genuinely difficult, and it is the part I would most want to
talk through. A test suite tells you the new engine does what you specified. It does not tell you
how the new engine differs from the one currently running on real patients.

<Flow steps={[
  { label: 'Candidate engine runs in shadow', detail: 'Passively, against live sessions. It decides nothing' },
  { label: 'Log every divergence', detail: 'Expected — a change usually exists to produce different output' },
  { label: 'Flag `new_looser` separately', detail: 'Any case where the new engine is LESS restrictive than the old. A disagreement is a review item; a safety regression is a release blocker', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Backtest against history', detail: 'Replay accumulated real sessions through the candidate before it sees live traffic' },
  { label: 'Release-blocking gate in CI', detail: 'In all three deployment workflows, with shared fixtures across the Go and TypeScript implementations' }
]} note="Separating 'different' from 'less safe' is what made the signal usable — otherwise a large intentional change buries the one divergence that matters." />

Reported on recent sessions: a complete match on recommended sets, with zero safety-looser
divergences.

Supporting this: high statement coverage on the engine, which is cheap to achieve on pure
decision logic and worth insisting on there specifically; decision records for each significant
revision; and a tracked log of gaps between the written clinical specification and the implemented
engine. Some revisions sat pending ethics-committee sign-off. Keeping the gaps in a file a
clinician could read beat quietly closing them.

## Related work in the same service

A combination-safety and substitution API: given a proposed regimen, check it for interactions,
and where a cheaper equivalent exists select one subject to contraindications and compatibility.
The conversation agent in the clinic's CRM consumes this rather than reasoning about
substitutions itself — the same principle enforced across a service boundary.

I also built a close-rate analysis tool that segments won and lost leads by routing outcome,
engine version and the flags raised during assessment, to test two hypotheses: that escalating a
case to doctor review suppresses conversion, and that leads drop off when moved between
platforms. It runs daily.

**No results are quoted, because none were recorded in a form I can verify.** The design I will
stand behind; the findings I would have to re-run.

## Stack

Go, PostgreSQL, hosted models through OpenRouter, OpenTelemetry, Docker, GitHub Actions.
